    "evidence_text": "There are few adverse drug reactions in these patients.\n\nPharmacological interventions for smoking cessation: an overview and network meta-analysis.\n\nBACKGROUND Smoking is the leading preventable cause of illness and premature death worldwide.\n\nHow do the remaining treatments compare with placebo in achieving long-term abstinence?\n\nMAIN RESULTS We identified 12 treatment-specific reviews.\n\nCombination NRT also outperformed single formulations.\n\nOther treatments failed to demonstrate a benefit compared with placebo.\n\nCombination NRT and varenicline are equally effective as quitting aids.\n\nNortriptyline also improves the chances of quitting.\n\nNortriptyline for smoking cessation: a review.\n\nDESIGN Systematic review with meta-analysis.   \n\n\nTopiramate for smoking cessation: a randomized, placebo-controlled pilot study.\n\nThe smoking quit date (QD) was scheduled after 2 weeks of medication treatment.\n\nRESULTS Fifty-seven subjects were randomized to treatment.\n\nA larger, PLC-controlled trial is needed to confirm these findings.\n\nOutcomes were biochemically confirmed.   \n\n\nNeither outcome was significantly different at 52 weeks.\n\nFurther research is required to determine the role of combination therapy in smoking cessation.   \n\n\nLong-term efficacy of nicotine replacement therapy for smoking cessation in adolescents: a randomized controlled trial.",
